  "worked_solution": "To obtain the given graph, we can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Reflect the graph in the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{-x}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238243/assets/30add2e841bb25faa97692ec22309482c9b18d8c55f878057978f0896f7420ea.png)\n\n- Reflect the graph in the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-x}.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238243/assets/0bab48c1fe5e6d1a0d90b1aae3c8cd123327d1b2e18ca8101e056726b76de002.png)\n\n- Translate the result to the right by $1$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}.$\n\n- Translate the result up by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}+4.$\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238243/assets/572a818b9621f8cec26f815429ff6d6e2846686afa348c7ae5a767dbd42fd3f6.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=-\\sqrt{-(x-1)}+4.$",
